Make fflush_stdout_and_exit() check stdout's error flag.
It is meant to exit with an error if stdout is in an error state, but
it only checked the result of fflush(). When an earlier write had
failed and left nothing in the buffer, fflush() succeeded and the
applet exited with its normal status. That is always the case for an
unbuffered stdout, so tee ignored every failed write to standard output
unless "-" was also given as a file, and it happens with the Microsoft
C runtime whenever a write goes straight to the file, as when standard
output is a closed descriptor ("uname >&-" exited with status 0). Check
ferror(stdout) as well.

---
 libbb/fflush_stdout_and_exit.c |    2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

--- a/libbb/fflush_stdout_and_exit.c
+++ b/libbb/fflush_stdout_and_exit.c
@@ -15,6 +15,8 @@
 {
 	if (fflush(stdout))
 		bb_simple_perror_msg_and_die(bb_msg_standard_output);
+	/* An earlier write may have failed with nothing left to flush */
+	die_if_ferror_stdout();
 	xfunc_error_retval = retval;
 	/* In case we are in NOFORK applet. Do not exit() directly,
 	 * but use xfunc_die() */
